{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EZTU4gy85yC1FPV6WZjUDa","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 5.431020511 angstrom 22.5 C\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant\"},{\"title\":\"A vacuum double-crystal spectrometer for reference-free highly charged ions X-ray spectroscopy\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.4520\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice constant\",\"url\":\"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"Template:Physical constants\",\"url\":\"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst\"},{\"title\":\"Loading AI tools\",\"url\":\"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"xray 2018\",\"url\":\"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Lattice spacing\",\"url\":\"https://demo.azizisearch.com/lite/wikipedia/page/Lattice_spacing\"},{\"title\":\"www.cambridge.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/advances-in-x-ray-analysis/article/abs/automated-lattice-parameter-determination-on-single-crystals/4C86BB2666640EA77D142697D63CBCE4\"},{\"title\":\"If the lattice constant of silicon is 5.43 Å, calculate?\",\"url\":\"https://pma.bosslaser.com/soluciones/14739717\"}]\n\nThe CODATA value you're asking about checks out. The 2018 CODATA X-ray values list the silicon lattice parameter at 22.5 °C in vacuum as a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m, with a relative uncertainty of 1.6×10⁻⁸. NIST's footnote says this is the unit-cell edge of an ideal natural silicon crystal, meaning one with no impurities or imperfections.\n\nThe CODATA 2022 values carry the same figure forward. A Wikipedia template that mirrors CODATA 2022 gives the silicon lattice parameter as a = 5.431020511(89)×10−10 m, so the value didn't change in the 2022 update.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- Measured values differ somewhat across sources. A research preprint notes that the variation of the d220 value between the 2006 and 2010 CODATA recommended values is more than 3 times the stated uncertainty. That preprint also gives a related reference-crystal spacing at 22.5 °C in vacuum of 192.0155696(10) pm.\n- A general reference table of lattice constants lists silicon as 5.431020511 Å, but its header gives values at 300 K, so it is not a 22.5 °C figure.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82463fa6-9dd8-4dde-b06a-fb4d94bf525a","uuid":"0105916c-8ad9-4692-b168-b3db3562c541","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:27:11.431Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CODATA lattice parameter of silicon 5.431020511 angstrom 22.5 C","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PAZ59jk4z4s8LHZJmX7WP6","content":[{"title":"Lattice constant","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_constant"},{"title":"A vacuum double-crystal spectrometer for reference-free highly charged ions X-ray spectroscopy","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.4520"},{"title":"Lattice constant","url":"https://Www.Wikipedia.org/wiki/Lattice_spacing"},{"title":"Template:Physical constants","url":"https://wiki.riteme.site/wiki/Template:Physconst"},{"title":"Loading AI tools","url":"https://wikiwand.com/en/Lattice_constants"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"xray 2018","url":"https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf"},{"title":"Lattice spacing","url":"https://demo.azizisearch.com/lite/wikipedia/page/Lattice_spacing"},{"title":"www.cambridge.org","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/advances-in-x-ray-analysis/article/abs/automated-lattice-parameter-determination-on-single-crystals/4C86BB2666640EA77D142697D63CBCE4"},{"title":"If the lattice constant of silicon is 5.43 Å, calculate?","url":"https://pma.bosslaser.com/soluciones/14739717"}]},"The CODATA value you're asking about checks out. The 2018 CODATA X-ray values list the silicon lattice parameter at 22.5 °C in vacuum as a 5.431 020 511(89)× 10−10 m, with a relative uncertainty of 1.6×10⁻⁸. NIST's footnote says this is the unit-cell edge of an ideal natural silicon crystal, meaning one with no impurities or imperfections.\n\nThe CODATA 2022 values carry the same figure forward. A Wikipedia template that mirrors CODATA 2022 gives the silicon lattice parameter as a = 5.431020511(89)×10−10 m, so the value didn't change in the 2022 update.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- Measured values differ somewhat across sources. A research preprint notes that the variation of the d220 value between the 2006 and 2010 CODATA recommended values is more than 3 times the stated uncertainty. That preprint also gives a related reference-crystal spacing at 22.5 °C in vacuum of 192.0155696(10) pm.\n- A general reference table of lattice constants lists silicon as 5.431020511 Å, but its header gives values at 300 K, so it is not a 22.5 °C figure."],"durationSeconds":5.756167720999991,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EZTU4gy85yC1FPV6WZjUDa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017FDYoNCRNjHnESBRg5MpAd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PBE lattice constant silicon diamond 5.47 Å benchmark germanium copper aluminum PBE lattice constants\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"QuantumATK: An integrated platform of electronic and atomic-scale modelling tools\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.02794\"},{\"title\":\"A benchmark of first-principles methods for accurate prediction of semiconductor band gaps\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.14644\"},{\"title\":\"Semiconductor Physics: A Density Functional Journey\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.13050\"},{\"title\":\"docs.quantumatk.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.quantumatk.com/tutorials/germanium/germanium.html\"},{\"title\":\"docs.quantumatk.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.quantumatk.com/tutorials/si50ge50/si50ge50.html\"},{\"title\":\"[Pw_forum] Lattice constant for copper\",\"url\":\"https://lists.quantum-espresso.org/pipermail/users/2011-March/019448.html\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.11177\"},{\"title\":\"docs.quantumatk.com\",\"url\":\"https://docs.quantumatk.com/tutorials/silicon/silicon.html\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1102.1358.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4753507/table/t3\"}]\n\nThe search didn't turn up a direct PBE lattice constant for silicon of 5.47 Å, so that value remains unconfirmed. It did find related benchmark data.\n\n- **Silicon:** One QuantumATK tutorial reports that the bare PBE functional overestimates silicon's lattice constant by 0.6%, and it cites an experimental value of 5.431 Å. That implies roughly 5.46 Å for PBE, close to your 5.47 Å figure. The PBEsol lattice constant for silicon is very close to the experimental value, 5.431 Å, but the pps-PBE method is also very accurate. The bare PBE functional (without any pseudopotential projector shifts) is known to overestimate lattice constants in general, but for silicon only by 0.6%.\n- **Germanium:** The same QuantumATK series reports that PBE overestimates the germanium lattice constant by more than 1% in the Si₀.₅Ge₀.₅ case, and it finds PBEsol closest to the experimental value of 5.6574 Å. The bare PBE functional (without any pseudopotential projector shifts) is known to overestimate lattice constants in general, and in this case it overestimates by more than 1%. A review-style paper says that the deviation is quite high, i.e., PBE over-binds the atoms so much.\n- **Copper:** A Quantum ESPRESSO forum post gives a published PBE value of 6.86 bohr (about 3.63 Å) and an experimental value of 6.83 bohr (about 3.61 Å). The post also reports the user's own calculation, which came out much lower, and asks why. This is a user query, not a validated benchmark.\n- **Aluminum:** I found no PBE aluminum lattice constant in these results.\n\nThe sources are mixed in quality. The QuantumATK pages are tutorial documentation, the forum post is an anecdote, and the review paper's wording is loose. For a rigorous PBE reference table, you would need a dedicated benchmark such as the Delta-test or a Materials Project comparison, which this search did not return.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"82463fa6-9dd8-4dde-b06a-fb4d94bf525a","uuid":"23525a5c-5414-485c-ba83-8f42d421a485","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:27:14.513Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"PBE lattice constant silicon diamond 5.47 Å benchmark germanium copper aluminum PBE lattice constants","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01V9eRpi76zBynT6vtBNz7CZ","content":[{"title":"QuantumATK: An integrated platform of electronic and atomic-scale modelling tools","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.02794"},{"title":"A benchmark of first-principles methods for accurate prediction of semiconductor band gaps","url":